log replication:

     • A server accepts AppendEntries requests from a leader that is not part of the server’s latest
       configuration. Otherwise, a new server could never be added to the cluster (it would never
       accept any log entries preceding the configuration entry that adds the server).

     • A server also grants its vote to a candidate that is not part of the server’s latest configuration (if
       the candidate has a sufficiently up-to-date log and a current term). This vote may occasionally
       be needed to keep the cluster available. For example, consider adding a fourth server to a
       three-server cluster. If one server were to fail, the new server’s vote would be needed to form
       a majority and elect a leader.

Thus, servers process incoming RPC requests without consulting their current configurations.

```

## Figure 4.1 (transcribed)

### AddServer RPC
Invoked by admin to add a server to the cluster configuration.
Arguments: newServer — address of server to add to configuration
Results: status — OK if server was added successfully; leaderHint — address of recent leader, if known
Receiver implementation:
1. Reply NOT_LEADER if not leader (§6.2)
2. Catch up new server for fixed number of rounds. Reply TIMEOUT if new server does not make progress for an election timeout or if the last round takes longer than the election timeout. (§4.2.1)
3. Wait until previous configuration in log is committed (§4.1)
4. Append new configuration entry to log (old configuration plus newServer), commit it using majority of new configuration (§4.1)
5. Reply OK

Assembler's note on step 2: if the new server's log already matches the leader's, catch-up is complete at once. Assembler's note on the wait step of both RPCs: if the log has no configuration entry, the initial configuration counts as committed.

### RemoveServer RPC
Invoked by admin to remove a server from the cluster configuration.
Arguments: oldServer — address of server to remove from configuration
Results: status — OK if server was removed successfully; leaderHint — address of recent leader, if known
Receiver implementation:
1. Reply NOT_LEADER if not leader (§6.2)
2. Wait until previous configuration in log is committed (§4.1)
3. Append new configuration entry to log (old configuration without oldServer), commit it using majority of new configuration (§4.1)
4. Reply OK and, if this server was removed, step down (§4.2.2)

Figure 4.1: RPCs used to change cluster membership. The AddServer RPC is used to add a new server to the current configuration, and the RemoveServer RPC is used to remove a server from the current configuration. Section numbers such as §4.1 indicate where particular features are discussed. Section 4.4 discusses ways to use these RPCs in a complete system.

## What the design does not include
(This section, the opening paragraph and the Source note are the steward's, not the dissertation's.)
Client requests other than commands and configuration changes; reads; snapshots or journal compaction; non-voting members. A command is an opaque payload appended by the coordinator on request.
# Replay scenario format (shared interface)

Version 1, fixed 2026-10-21. Both sides compile a scenario: the model side into a TLC configuration that constrains the environment to the scenario's steps while exploring every interleaving it leaves open; the implementation side into fault injection and requests against a running system, sampled over seeds.

## Shape
```yaml
scenario: <name>
derives: [J1.5, G1.3]           # clauses and cells exercised
initial: [m1, m2, m3, m4]       # initial configuration; m5 exists too and is started with it
steps:
  - at: <trigger>
    do: <action>
end: {settle: 100}               # events after the last step's action
bound: {events: 400}
```

## Triggers
`start`, or an observable event from `replay/contract.md` with optional field filters, for example `{event: Elected, member: m2}`, `{event: Committed, member: m2, index: 3}`, `{event: Appended, member: m5, kind: config}`. Step 1 is armed at the start; step *i* is armed when step *i*−1's action is taken, and matches only events emitted after it was armed (the action's own synchronous events count). Once a trigger fires, the action is taken at some later point: the model explores every such point, the implementation samples one.

## Actions
```yaml
do: {add: m5, at: m1}                 # an administrator asks m1 to add m5 (G1.4)
do: {remove: m4, at: m2}              # asks m2 to remove m4 (G1.4)
do: {crash: m3}                       # G1.1
do: {restart: m3}                     # G1.1
do: {cut: [[m1, m2], [m1, m3]]}       # each listed link down in both directions (G1.3)
do: {drop: [[m2, m1]]}                # messages from m2 to m1 lost; the reverse untouched (G1.3)
do: {heal: true}                      # every link restored
do: {command: c1, at: m1}             # an administrator asks m1 to append command c1; a member that is not coordinator ignores it
```
Three selectors may stand for a member name anywhere one is allowed, resolved when the step's trigger fires: `coordinator`, the member most recently `Elected`; `follower`, a member that is up and is not `coordinator` (the model explores every such member, the implementation picks the lowest-numbered); `crashed`, the member most recently crashed by a `crash` step (a scenario that uses it before any crash is rejected; likewise `coordinator` before any `Elected`). A self-pair in `cut` or `drop` is ignored; link failures accumulate until `heal`.

## End and bound
`end: {settle: N}`: the run continues for N events after the last step's action is taken, then stops. A violation of J1.1, J1.3 or J1.5 witnessed at any point is `FAIL <clause>`. If `bound` is reached before every step's action is taken, the outcome is `UNEXPLAINED steps` (the scenario never reached its point), never PASS. Otherwise the outcome is `PASS`. The model side explores every behaviour within its bounds that takes every step and at most N further events, with every prefix of such behaviours; it reports `UNEXPLAINED steps` if no behaviour within its bounds takes every step.

## Where scenarios live
Shared scenarios are in `replay/scenarios/<name>.yaml`. Each side's runner also accepts a path to a scenario file anywhere, because the steward may run scenarios the authors have not seen.
# Replay observability schema (shared interface)

Version 1, fixed 2026-10-21. Both sides write one JSON object per line to `<side>/observe/<run>.jsonl`; the model side from a TLC trace, the implementation side live.

```json
{"proc": "m2", "seq": 14, "event": "Committed", "index": 3, "epoch": 2,
 "causes": [{"proc": "m2", "seq": 12}], "run": "<run id>", "side": "impl"}
```
- `proc`, `seq`: the member the event concerns and its own counter. There is no cross-member clock; wall time, if kept, goes in a separate file for humans.
- Event fields are exactly those in `replay/contract.md`.
- `causes`: the records this one follows from, by `(proc, seq)`. An `Appended` on a follower names the coordinator's `Appended` of the same entry; a `Committed` names the `Appended` of the entry committed; an `Elected` names the ballot grants that elected it, recorded as the voters' `(proc, seq)` of their latest record when they granted; a `ConfigInEffect` names the `Appended` or `Truncated` that caused it.
- Fault steps are recorded as `Fault` records from `proc: "env"` with the step's action as fields and, for `cut` and `drop`, the resolved directed links.

## The question a trace must answer
For any violation of J1.1 or J1.5: which configuration entries each involved member had in effect at each election and each commitment, and which members' grants or acknowledgements those decisions counted. A trace that cannot answer this has failed the observability layer's own test.

